WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.740
How was it?

00:00:01.740 --> 00:00:02.780
You were great in that.

00:00:02.780 --> 00:00:04.010
What was it like,
Thank you.

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:07.350
popping into that movie,
in "Inglorious Basterds"?

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:10.320
I think, as you were describing,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.870
like if somebody called me
to play Colonel Sanders.

00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:16.890
I got a call, "Quentin
Tarantino wants to talk to you."

00:00:16.890 --> 00:00:18.530
I was saying, "Okay."

00:00:18.530 --> 00:00:20.770
Again, thought it was my brother, Paul.

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.370
Picked it up, it was Quentin Tarantino,

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:25.387
and he said he's doing a
movie about World War II,

00:00:25.387 --> 00:00:27.560
"would you want to play
a British general?"

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:30.807
And I was like, "Yeah."
(chuckles)

00:00:30.807 --> 00:00:33.057
"Of course I want to
play a British general."

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:35.420
And we talked about World War II movies.

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:37.080
We were supposed to talk for 45 minutes,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.000
we ended up talking for eight hours about

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:40.833
Come on.

00:00:40.833 --> 00:00:41.750
various war movies.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.570
'Cause I love war movies.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:46.920
I wouldn't want to actually be in a war.

00:00:46.920 --> 00:00:48.400
Just like I don't think anybody wants

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.170
to be in an actual horror film situation.

00:00:51.170 --> 00:00:54.190
But both my parents were in World War II.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.530
My dad was in the Royal Engineers,

00:00:55.530 --> 00:00:57.980
my mom was in the Royal Air Force,

00:00:57.980 --> 00:01:00.723
and it shaped who they are.

00:01:02.637 --> 00:01:05.310
And the Nazis were just bad guys,

00:01:05.310 --> 00:01:08.740
just clearly Nazis, fascists,

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.830
horrible, horrible people that are...

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:15.470
They're the bad guys.

00:01:17.365 --> 00:01:20.540
So, for me, all the
World War II movies are

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:24.750
so, so clear of being on the right side

00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:28.820
and fighting the good fight.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:31.110
So, he called me up...

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.210
So I'd become a World War II aficionado,

00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:36.163
and he blew my mind cause he said,

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.750
because he knew exactly what he was doing,

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:40.480
which is so fascinating.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:42.250
And I have such a respect for him,

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.740
and I just think he's a hero.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.070
But he said, just like
with spaghetti westerns,

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.810
he said, "I'm making what's
called a macaroni combat."

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.850
That's what the subgenre is.

00:01:56.850 --> 00:01:59.330
Which is to say that
it's a European knockoff

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:00.800
of an American war movie,

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.568
which is like (sighs) heaven, heaven.

00:02:04.568 --> 00:02:05.401
(chuckles)

00:02:05.401 --> 00:02:07.500
And he said that the
term "macaroni combat"

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.130
is actually Japanglish.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.130
It's because Japanese film critics

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:14.597
had coined that term of "macaroni combat."

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:19.610
And he talked about this
kind of magical reality

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:20.443
he wanted to create.

00:02:20.443 --> 00:02:22.190
And I was like, "Okay, that sounds good.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:24.100
I wonder how that translates to the page."

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:25.130
Then I'm reading the script,

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.820
and I'm like, "Holy shit,
they fucking kill Hitler.

00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:28.920
They kill Hitler."

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.140
I couldn't believe it.
I'm like, "I am so in."

00:02:32.140 --> 00:02:34.260
And on top of it, it is
that thing that I love,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:35.623
that kind of British, like,

00:02:36.977 --> 00:02:39.210
"We're here. The enemy's there."

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:40.207
You know?

00:02:40.207 --> 00:02:42.164
"Your job..." you know, that whole thing.

00:02:42.164 --> 00:02:43.182
Yeah. (chuckles)
And I was just like...

00:02:43.182 --> 00:02:44.810
(stammering)

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:46.210
And I loved every second of it.

00:02:46.210 --> 00:02:49.050
Shot in Nazi headquarters.

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.580
No. Really?

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:52.993
Yeah. It was all this Albert Speer,

00:02:54.322 --> 00:02:58.013
I think it's Potsdam, was in
Berlin anyways, in Germany,

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:01.830
and everything in threes,

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.700
the type of architecture
that makes you feel

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.980
that the state is more
powerful than you, you know,

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.690
all that "ein Volk,
ein Reich, ein Führer,"

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:13.263
crazy, oversized doors
that are intimidating.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:19.590
It then became a Soviet army headquarters.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.450
And then the Americans,

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:23.490
when the Berlin Wall fell,
the Americans had it,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:25.290
and now they rent it out for movies.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.550
My first day, I get there,

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:30.250
and I'm wearing my British army uniform,

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:31.930
and I'm just thrilled, you know?

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:34.610
I'm in this room filled
with thousands and thousands

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:35.950
of World War II uniforms.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:38.420
This is my dream come true.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:42.613
And he goes, "You know,
Quentin wants to see you."

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.500
So I go over there, and it's the scene

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:51.472
where Hitler is getting his portrait done.

00:03:51.472 --> 00:03:53.150
(laughing)
So I'm sitting there,

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.850
and then Quentin Tarantino
was like, "And action!"

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.852
And then Hitler's leg, you know,

00:03:58.852 --> 00:04:02.940
(speaking foreign language)

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:04.679
you know, the whole thing.

00:04:04.679 --> 00:04:06.830
And I'm like, "This is a fever dream.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:08.500
This is a fever dream."

00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:09.480
You know?

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:12.940
I'm in the place where,

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:16.320
I think Leni Riefenstahl, had an office.

00:04:16.320 --> 00:04:18.083
Like this giant...
Right.

00:04:19.491 --> 00:04:21.030
No, no, no. That's where
I picked up my uniform.

00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:22.650
And then I'm in Nazi headquarters,

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.530
and it's just like Gestapo
headquarters or whatever.

00:04:26.530 --> 00:04:30.060
And there's a guy dressed as
Hitler wearing a wrestling cape

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.650
and a French guy painting him
with like a mais-oui beret

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.080
and a mustache, and then it's "and cut!"

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.397
And then Tarantino turns around

00:04:38.397 --> 00:04:39.474
and goes, "What do you think?"

00:04:39.474 --> 00:04:41.760
I go, "What do you think what do I think?"

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.760
I'm like, "This is every
Airfix 132nd toy soldier,

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:50.870
every Spitfire..."

00:04:50.870 --> 00:04:54.470
I've made over 700 1/32nd scale,

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:57.730
1/72nd scale Spitfires in my life.

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:01.460
Every story of my parents
talking about the Blitz

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.450
and my dad being in the Black Forest

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:07.080
in the second wave of
Operation Market Garden.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:08.580
It was just a dream come true.

